CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 5 ----------
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Distinguish a program from a process.", "A program is a passive file on disk. A process is that program in execution plus every piece of state the execution needs and every resource it holds. One program can have many processes; the file is a recipe, the process is the cooking."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "List what the kernel keeps for a process.", "Identity (pid, ppid, group, session); saved registers; a memory map and page tables; the open file descriptor table; credentials; signal state; filesystem context (cwd, root, umask); scheduling state; resource limits and accounting; and namespace and cgroup pointers. In Linux this is <code>struct task_struct</code>, about 10 KB."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Why must <code>cd</code> be a shell builtin rather than a program?", "The current working directory is per-process kernel state. A child process cannot change its parent's, so a separate <code>cd</code> program would change its own directory and exit."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "What do the process states R, S, D, T and Z mean?", "R running or runnable; S interruptible sleep (waiting, wakeable by a signal — where most processes are); D uninterruptible sleep (inside the kernel at a point where waking would corrupt state); T stopped by a signal; Z zombie — finished but its exit status not yet collected."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Why can you not kill a process in D state, even with <code>kill -9</code>?", "It is inside the kernel holding state that signal delivery would corrupt, typically partway through a disk or network filesystem operation. It leaves D when the operation completes; if it never completes — a hung NFS mount, a detached block device — it never leaves. Fix the device or reboot."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Why can a machine with idle processors show a load average of 40?", "Linux load average counts running <em>and</em> uninterruptibly-sleeping tasks. A stuck storage device puts many tasks in D state. Load average is not a CPU metric on Linux."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "What is a zombie, why can you not kill one, and how do you clear them?", "A finished process whose <code>task_struct</code> remains because its parent has not called <code>wait()</code>. It is already dead — you cannot kill it. You fix or kill the parent, at which point PID 1 inherits and reaps them. A loop that never reaps eventually exhausts the PID space."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "What happens to children when their parent dies?", "They are reparented to PID 1 (or the nearest subreaper) and keep running. This is how a process survives the terminal that started it."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Why did Unix split process creation into <code>fork</code> and <code>execve</code>?", "The gap between them is a place where the child can adjust itself with ordinary calls — redirect output, drop privileges, change directory, close descriptors, join namespaces — before the new program starts. Shell redirection, pipes and container setup all live in that gap."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Because a Node server is one process, name four things every request handler shares.", "One file descriptor table (so all connections share the limit), one address space (so a leak anywhere affects everything), one set of credentials, and one cgroup budget. Plus: a native crash takes all in-flight requests. That blast radius, not raw throughput, is the honest reason to run several processes."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Why do cluster workers share no cache, no counter and no session state?", "Because separate processes have separate address spaces by definition. That single fact forces Redis into more architectures than any other consideration."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "How does <code>cluster</code> let several processes accept on one port?", "The primary creates the listening socket and passes the descriptor to the children over a Unix socket — possible because a descriptor is a per-process number pointing at a kernel object. Since Node 0.12 the primary round-robins connections, because kernel-level distribution was badly unbalanced."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Contrast cluster with <code>worker_threads</code> in one sentence each.", "Cluster gives isolation: separate processes, separate heaps, separate everything, a crash contained. Workers give sharing: one process, one descriptor table, one PID, separate V8 isolates but transferable buffers and <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> — and a V8 crash kills everything."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Why is your container's entrypoint special?", "It is PID 1 in its PID namespace, so the kernel does not deliver signals with default actions to it, and it is expected to reap orphans. A plain <code>node index.js</code> with no <code>SIGTERM</code> handler ignores <code>docker stop</code> and is <code>SIGKILL</code>ed ten seconds later."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Why do <code>--init</code> and <code>tini</code> exist?", "Because Node does not reap orphans, and PID 1 is expected to. A container that shells out to subprocesses accumulates zombies without a proper init as PID 1."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Define a Kubernetes pod in terms of a process's state.", "A set of containers sharing some parts of that state — the network namespace (hence <code>localhost</code>, hence they cannot both bind port 3000) and optionally the PID namespace — but not the filesystem unless a volume is mounted into both."),
("node-5", "Node 5 · The process", "Name five files under <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/</code> and what each answers.", "<code>status</code> (state, threads, RSS, context switches), <code>fd/</code> (every open descriptor as a symlink — the leak diagnosis), <code>maps</code> (the address space), <code>limits</code> (effective resource limits), <code>cmdline</code> (arguments). Reading those five tells you more in thirty seconds than most instrumentation."),
# ---------- Node 6 ----------
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "Why is a process unable to detect that it was context-switched?", "Its entire execution state is its registers, and they were restored exactly. It has no mechanism by which it could notice — only by reading a clock and seeing time passed."),
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "List the steps of a context switch.", "Enter the kernel (always — via syscall, interrupt or timer); save the user registers; save the kernel-side state into the outgoing task; choose the next task; load its page table root into <code>cr3</code>; switch kernel stacks and restore its registers; return to user mode."),
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "What is the direct cost of a context switch, and what is the indirect cost?", "Direct: roughly 1–3 µs of instructions. Indirect and larger: the incoming process finds cold L1/L2 caches, a disturbed TLB, and branch predictors describing the wrong program — so it runs at a fraction of full speed until they refill. Several to tens of microseconds all-in."),
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "Why is a thread switch cheaper than a process switch — precisely which step is skipped?", "The <code>cr3</code> load. Threads share an address space, so the page table does not change, the TLB is not disturbed, and the caches likely still hold shared data. That is the entire difference."),
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "What do voluntary and nonvoluntary context switch counts tell you?", "Voluntary means the process gave up the processor because it had nothing to do — normal and high for an I/O-bound service. Nonvoluntary means the kernel took it away because a slice expired or something more urgent appeared — a rising count means it wants more CPU than it is getting, from competition or a cgroup quota. Both in <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/status</code>."),
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "Give the arithmetic argument for the event loop.", "Thread-per-connection pays a context switch per event: microseconds, with cold caches, plus ~100 KB of memory per connection. One thread handling many connections replaces the switch with a <em>function call</em>: nanoseconds, warm caches, no page table change. Three orders of magnitude, and that is the whole thesis."),
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "What does the event loop's advantage explicitly not claim?", "Anything about being faster at computation. It wins on concurrency of waiting and loses on parallelism of computing."),
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "Why is <code>max_connections</code> in Postgres typically 100–200 rather than 10,000?", "Postgres uses one OS process per connection, so each costs a <code>task_struct</code>, several megabytes of private memory, and a scheduler entry. A few hundred is a heavy load on the machine. Hence connection pools, and hence pgbouncer multiplexing thousands of clients onto dozens of real connections."),
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "Why does raising <code>UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE</code> above your core count not add capacity for crypto work?", "More runnable threads than cores means the kernel rotates them, paying the full switch cost while doing no extra work. The correct bound is cores for CPU-bound work; it may exceed cores for genuinely blocking work. Tell them apart by whether the threads sit in R or in D."),
("node-6", "Node 6 · The context switch", "What does the <code>cs</code> column in <code>vmstat 1</code> indicate when it is very high?", "Hundreds of thousands of switches per second on a machine that is not obviously busy means thrashing: too many threads, lock contention, or a poll loop with a tiny timeout."),
# ---------- Node 7 ----------
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "What single piece of hardware makes preemption possible?", "A programmable interval timer raising an interrupt at a fixed rate. Control then enters the kernel regardless of what the program was doing, and the program cannot prevent it because <code>cli</code> is privileged."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "What is the historical difference between cooperative and preemptive multitasking?", "Cooperative (classic Mac OS through 9, Windows 3.x) meant a program kept the processor until it yielded, so one bad application froze the machine. Preemptive means the kernel can take it back. Everything mainstream has been preemptive for twenty-five years — except the JavaScript event loop."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "Name the five scheduling goals that conflict.", "Throughput (favours long slices), latency (favours short ones), fairness, priority (opposes fairness), and interactivity (a process that just got a keystroke should run now despite having no fairness claim)."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "What did CTSS's multi-level feedback queue infer, and how?", "Which processes are interactive — from behaviour rather than declaration. A process using its whole slice is moved down (compute-bound, longer slice, less often); one that blocks early stays high. MIT, ~1961, Corbató."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "Explain CFS in one sentence, plus how it handles wake-ups.", "Every task carries a <code>vruntime</code> scaled by its weight, tasks live in a red-black tree ordered by it, and the scheduler always runs the smallest; a waking task is given a <code>vruntime</code> just below the tree's minimum so it runs almost immediately — interactivity falls out of the model rather than being a special case."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "What does one <code>nice</code> level change?", "Roughly ten percent of processor share — each step scales the task's weight by about 1.25. Range −20 to +19. <code>nice -n 19 npm run build</code> on a machine also serving traffic is a real and underused move."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "Name Linux's scheduling classes in priority order.", "<code>SCHED_DEADLINE</code>, then the real-time classes <code>SCHED_FIFO</code>/<code>SCHED_RR</code> (priorities 1–99, which preempt everything below and can hang a machine — hence <code>sched_rt_runtime_us</code> reserving 5%), then <code>SCHED_OTHER</code> (fair share, where everything you run lives), then <code>SCHED_BATCH</code> and <code>SCHED_IDLE</code>."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "Why are there per-core run queues, and what problem does that create?", "A single global queue would need a lock contended by every core on every decision. Per-core queues mean cores can idle while another is backed up, so a load balancer migrates tasks — reluctantly, because migration costs cache locality, and on a NUMA machine crossing sockets can halve memory performance."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "What is priority inversion, and what real incident is the standard example?", "A low-priority task holds a lock a high-priority task needs, and a medium-priority task preempts the low one — so the high-priority task is blocked by one it outranks. Mars Pathfinder, July 1997, resetting on the surface. The fix uploaded to the spacecraft was priority inheritance."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "Correct the claim that Node uses one core.", "Your JavaScript runs on one thread, so one Node process executes at most one of your functions at a time. The process also has libuv threadpool threads and V8 compiler and GC threads, which the kernel scheduler will run on other cores — so a Node process routinely exceeds 100% CPU."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "Explain the two-stacked-schedulers framing.", "The kernel scheduler is preemptive and arbitrates between processes and threads; the event loop is a scheduler too, deciding which callback runs next, but it is cooperative and regains control only when a callback returns. A 400 ms synchronous handler is a failure of the second, not the first — which is why the machine stays healthy while every other request in that process waits."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "In what sense is React's concurrent renderer this node reimplemented?", "It slices work into units, yields to the browser roughly every 5 ms via <code>shouldYield()</code>, assigns work to lanes by priority, and lets a keystroke interrupt a low-priority render. <code>startTransition</code> is <code>nice</code>; <code>requestIdleCallback</code> is <code>SCHED_IDLE</code>; <code>scheduler.postTask</code> is priority scheduling on the platform."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "Distinguish a Kubernetes CPU request from a CPU limit at the kernel level.", "A request becomes a cgroup <em>weight</em> — a relative share that only matters under contention. A limit becomes a <em>quota</em>: at most N ms of CPU per 100 ms period, enforced by stopping every thread in the container until the next period, even on an idle machine."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "What is the symptom of CPU throttling, and where is the evidence?", "Latency spikes at moderate average utilisation with no corresponding CPU graph. Evidence: <code>nr_throttled</code> and <code>throttled_usec</code> climbing in <code>/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat</code>, and rising <code>nonvoluntary_ctxt_switches</code>."),
("node-7", "Node 7 · The scheduler", "Why does <code>os.cpus().length</code> lie inside a container?", "<code>/proc/cpuinfo</code> is not namespaced, so it reports the host's cores. Limits live in cgroup files, not in <code>/proc</code>. Use <code>os.availableParallelism()</code> (Node 18.14+)."),
# ---------- Node 8 ----------
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "Why do two computers left alone disagree about the time?", "Every clock descends from a quartz crystal accurate to perhaps 20 parts per million — about 1.7 seconds of drift per day — and its rate changes with temperature. Disagreement is physical, not a software defect."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "Name the machine's clock sources and what each is for.", "The battery-backed RTC, read once at boot to initialise the system clock; the PIT and HPET, programmable timers that raise interrupts; and the TSC, a cycle counter readable in a couple of nanoseconds without entering the kernel, used for all high-resolution timing once processors gained an invariant, cross-core-synchronised version."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "What is a tickless kernel and why did it happen?", "Rather than a fixed HZ drumbeat, the kernel programs the timer for whenever the next thing is actually due. A fixed 1000 Hz tick meant a thousand interrupts a second even when idle — bad for battery, and catastrophic on a virtualisation host running two hundred idle guests."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "Distinguish <code>CLOCK_REALTIME</code> from <code>CLOCK_MONOTONIC</code>.", "<code>CLOCK_REALTIME</code> is wall-clock time since the 1970 epoch and <strong>can jump forwards or backwards</strong> when an administrator or NTP corrects it. <code>CLOCK_MONOTONIC</code> only increases and is meaningless as a date. Durations must use the monotonic one."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "Why is <code>Date.now() - start</code> a bug?", "It is a wall clock. An NTP correction mid-measurement can make the duration wrong or negative, which then flows into code assuming non-negative. Use <code>performance.now()</code> or <code>process.hrtime.bigint()</code>."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "What is the difference between slewing and stepping a clock?", "Stepping jumps to the correct value, making time discontinuous. Slewing changes the clock's rate slightly until it catches up, keeping it monotonic. Standard practice is slew small corrections, step large ones; <code>chrony</code> converges much faster than the original <code>ntpd</code>, which matters on virtual machines."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "What happened on 30 June 2012 and what is leap smearing?", "A leap second triggered a Linux <code>hrtimer</code> livelock spiking CPU to 100% across huge numbers of machines and hanging Java applications; Reddit, Mozilla and Qantas's booking system went down. Leap smearing, now widespread, spreads the extra second over a 24-hour window as a tiny rate change so no discontinuity occurs."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "What is the vDSO and what does it explain about <code>strace</code>?", "A page of kernel-maintained code and data mapped read-only into every process, so <code>clock_gettime</code> reads the TSC and applies the parameters entirely in user mode, at ~20 ns and with no system call. It is why <code>Date.now()</code> shows no syscall in <code>strace</code>, and it appears as <code>[vdso]</code> in <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/maps</code>."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "What exactly does <code>setTimeout(fn, 1000)</code> promise?", "A minimum, never a maximum. The timer fires only when the loop reaches its timers phase, so a running callback delays it, machine load delays it, and kernel granularity adds more. 1400 ms under load is not a bug."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "Why is <code>setTimeout(fn, 0)</code> not immediate?", "It is clamped — 1 ms in Node, and in browsers 4 ms once five nested timeouts have accumulated, a rule from 2010 because sites used it as a busy loop. For after-this-callback use a microtask; for after-I/O use <code>setImmediate</code>."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "What happens to timers in a background browser tab?", "Throttled to roughly once per second, and since Chrome 88 to once per minute after five minutes hidden. A <code>setInterval</code> poll stops and then bursts on return — which is why <code>visibilitychange</code>, SSE/WebSocket push, and <code>refetchOnWindowFocus</code> exist."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "Contrast the failure modes of <code>setInterval</code> and a self-rescheduling <code>setTimeout</code>.", "<code>setInterval</code> schedules from a fixed base, so async work taking longer than the interval produces overlapping executions — two requests in flight, or double processing. A <code>setTimeout</code> rescheduled at the end of the work cannot overlap but drifts by the work's duration each round. Choose knowing which failure you prefer."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "Why does a JWT library have a <code>clockTolerance</code> option?", "Because <code>nbf</code> and <code>exp</code> are wall-clock seconds and the issuing and verifying servers' clocks differ. A token issued by a server 30 seconds ahead is not yet valid to the verifier, failing with an error that reads like a code bug. TOTP and TLS certificate validation have the same problem."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "Distinguish Postgres <code>now()</code> from <code>clock_timestamp()</code>, and <code>timestamp</code> from <code>timestamptz</code>.", "<code>now()</code> returns the transaction start time and does not change within it; <code>clock_timestamp()</code> reads the actual clock each call. <code>timestamptz</code> stores an absolute instant in UTC; <code>timestamp</code> stores a wall-clock reading with no zone, which becomes unrecoverably ambiguous at a daylight-saving transition."),
("node-8", "Node 8 · Time", "Why can timestamps not order events across machines, and what is used instead?", "Clocks differ by milliseconds at best, so A's 10:00:00.100 and B's 10:00:00.050 tell you nothing about order. Systems use logical clocks (Lamport, vector), hybrid logical clocks, or Spanner's TrueTime, which returns a bounded interval and waits out the uncertainty. Practically: database sequences or ULID/Snowflake IDs, never client timestamps, and never last-write-wins by wall clock unless you accept discarding newer writes."),
]
